WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.501 --> 00:00:03.378
When you were 15 years old

00:00:03.378 --> 00:00:06.214
what job did you expect to have 
as a 30 year old?

00:00:06.464 --> 00:00:07.590
—Writer.
—Singer.

00:00:07.590 --> 00:00:08.508
Teacher.

00:00:08.508 --> 00:00:11.594
I asked this question to colleagues,
but for more than 20 years...

00:00:11.886 --> 00:00:13.596
that question has also been asked

00:00:13.596 --> 00:00:15.640
to millions of teenagers worldwide.

00:00:16.224 --> 00:00:18.810
What job do you expect to be doing at 30?

00:00:18.935 --> 00:00:19.686
Lawyer.

00:00:19.686 --> 00:00:22.605
—Journalist.
—Managing a production company.

00:00:23.023 --> 00:00:25.442
This question is important because 
over time it can tell us

00:00:25.442 --> 00:00:28.028
how a generation of young people 
thinks about the future.

00:00:29.195 --> 00:00:31.531
So how do actually 15 year olds answer?

00:00:32.365 --> 00:00:33.533
Let's find out.

00:00:36.745 --> 00:00:38.913
This visualization shows all the jobs

00:00:38.913 --> 00:00:41.416
15 year olds listed 
in the year 2000.

00:00:42.292 --> 00:00:45.670
The top job was medical doctor, 
but there's also athlete...

00:00:46.296 --> 00:00:48.548
Decorator, military officer...

00:00:49.215 --> 00:00:50.675
Way lower on the list

00:00:50.675 --> 00:00:54.345
there are jobs like pharmacist 
and real estate agent.

00:00:55.472 --> 00:00:56.806
There are so many jobs listed

00:00:56.806 --> 00:00:59.893
that researchers tried to simplify it 
by clustering the jobs

00:00:59.893 --> 00:01:01.519
into even broader categories.

00:01:01.895 --> 00:01:03.938
Here's the top ten list for girls.

00:01:04.439 --> 00:01:09.194
Nearly half of all kids expected to do 
the same ten jobs.

00:01:10.153 --> 00:01:11.654
That was more than 20 years ago.

00:01:11.654 --> 00:01:13.656
So these kids are now in their mid-30s.

00:01:14.157 --> 00:01:16.284
Did their expectations come true?

00:01:16.785 --> 00:01:17.577
Not really.

00:01:17.577 --> 00:01:21.456
For example, about 11% of girls 
and about 5% of boys

00:01:21.456 --> 00:01:23.416
expected to be doctors.

00:01:23.416 --> 00:01:27.128
But these days, less than 0.5% of
working age people

00:01:27.128 --> 00:01:29.214
in the countries where they did this survey

00:01:29.214 --> 00:01:31.049
are actually doctors.

00:01:32.467 --> 00:01:35.553
In 2018, this question was asked 
again to 15 year olds...

00:01:35.678 --> 00:01:38.264
and this time to a much bigger group.

00:01:38.556 --> 00:01:40.850
Again, the top answers were doctor...

00:01:41.351 --> 00:01:42.477
lawyer...

00:01:43.061 --> 00:01:43.853
athlete.

00:01:44.604 --> 00:01:46.773
And there were a bunch of other 
interesting jobs, too.

00:01:48.191 --> 00:01:50.235
When researchers simplified this list...

00:01:50.652 --> 00:01:54.155
half of all kids expected to do 
the same ten jobs again.

00:01:55.198 --> 00:01:57.575
But from 2000 to 2018

00:01:57.575 --> 00:01:59.619
one thing did change.

00:01:59.619 --> 00:02:02.914
In 2000, a portion of kids didn't actually 
name a job.

00:02:03.164 --> 00:02:04.833
About 14%.

00:02:05.250 --> 00:02:08.795
In 2018, that group jumped 
to 25%.

00:02:09.754 --> 00:02:11.339
Here's why that's a problem.

00:02:13.133 --> 00:02:14.384
There's a great piece of research

00:02:14.384 --> 00:02:17.345
that tracked thousands of 8th graders 
starting in 1988.

00:02:17.971 --> 00:02:19.848
They specifically focused on kids

00:02:19.848 --> 00:02:23.393
who had science related ambitions
but were just average in math.

00:02:24.352 --> 00:02:25.728
They compared that group to kids

00:02:25.728 --> 00:02:27.772
who had no science-related ambitions...

00:02:28.106 --> 00:02:29.691
but they were high achieving in math.

00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:32.902
Turns out the kids with 
science-related ambitions...

00:02:33.153 --> 00:02:35.989
were far more likely to get 
a science or engineering degree...

00:02:36.281 --> 00:02:38.074
even though they weren't as good at math.

00:02:38.658 --> 00:02:41.786
What this study showed was that ambition,
even at an early age...

00:02:42.078 --> 00:02:44.873
plays a huge part in people's 
professional outcomes.

00:02:45.915 --> 00:02:47.250
Part of why it's so important

00:02:47.250 --> 00:02:49.460
is that it helps them plan for the future.

00:02:50.086 --> 00:02:50.795
For example

00:02:50.795 --> 00:02:53.673
if they expect to have a job that 
requires a college degree...

00:02:54.007 --> 00:02:56.259
then they should make plans to go to college.

00:02:56.593 --> 00:02:58.970
For that reason, the PISA survey
also asks

00:02:58.970 --> 00:03:01.431
if kids expect to attend college.

00:03:02.098 --> 00:03:04.184
And it found something interesting.

00:03:04.184 --> 00:03:06.269
Among the kids who expect 
to have a job

00:03:06.269 --> 00:03:08.062
that requires a college degree...

00:03:08.563 --> 00:03:11.566
1 in 5 had no plans to attend college...

00:03:12.066 --> 00:03:14.527
which means they probably 
won't be doing the job

00:03:14.527 --> 00:03:16.237
they expect to do.

00:03:16.571 --> 00:03:18.615
When teens expect 
to have a certain job

00:03:18.615 --> 00:03:21.910
but they don't plan on getting 
the required education or experience

00:03:21.910 --> 00:03:24.037
researchers who study career paths

00:03:24.037 --> 00:03:25.663
call that misalignment.

00:03:26.539 --> 00:03:28.416
One study of Australian teenagers

00:03:28.416 --> 00:03:30.960
found that kids who are misaligned at 15

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:33.087
were twice as likely to be unemployed

00:03:33.087 --> 00:03:34.964
or not in school at age 25.

00:03:36.216 --> 00:03:38.509
Another study tracked 
American 15 year olds.

00:03:38.885 --> 00:03:41.846
They found that the students who were 
aligned or over aligned

00:03:41.846 --> 00:03:43.473
which means they plan to get more

00:03:43.473 --> 00:03:45.642
than enough education for 
their expected job...

00:03:46.100 --> 00:03:48.436
These kids had higher wages 
throughout their career

00:03:48.436 --> 00:03:51.481
compared to misaligned kids.

00:03:52.815 --> 00:03:55.235
But researchers who study
these career pathways

00:03:55.235 --> 00:03:58.071
have a pretty good idea of things 
we can do to help students

00:03:58.071 --> 00:04:00.490
think more clearly about their future.

00:04:00.490 --> 00:04:03.368
For one, young people need to 
explore various careers...

00:04:03.785 --> 00:04:05.828
maybe by visiting workplaces.

00:04:05.828 --> 00:04:07.997
And it's useful if they've 
experienced a workplace

00:04:07.997 --> 00:04:10.875
by working part-time 
or volunteering.

00:04:10.875 --> 00:04:13.002
Those experiences should help them

00:04:13.002 --> 00:04:15.338
think about what job they 
expect to do 

00:04:15.338 --> 00:04:16.631
and make plans accordingly.

00:04:17.298 --> 00:04:19.634
But most students don't get these opportunities.

00:04:20.468 --> 00:04:22.887
Only about 40% of students 
in this survey

00:04:22.887 --> 00:04:26.015
said they participated in job shadowing 
or worksite visits.

00:04:26.474 --> 00:04:27.850
And a similar percentage of kids

00:04:27.850 --> 00:04:29.936
said they participated in job fairs.

00:04:30.436 --> 00:04:32.105
In other words, 15 year olds

00:04:32.105 --> 00:04:34.482
aren't getting the support they need 
to answer this question.

00:04:35.400 --> 00:04:37.151
When we don't do the work of helping kids

00:04:37.151 --> 00:04:38.611
think through their futures

00:04:38.611 --> 00:04:40.780
it's disadvantaged kids who suffer the most.

00:04:41.739 --> 00:04:43.574
Among the most affluent kids

00:04:43.574 --> 00:04:45.910
only about 1 in 10 are misaligned.

00:04:46.577 --> 00:04:48.496
But among the poorest kids

00:04:48.496 --> 00:04:50.707
about 1 in 3 are misaligned.

00:04:51.374 --> 00:04:54.627
So we entrench the inequality 
that already exists.

00:04:57.130 --> 00:05:00.049
This is every kid who answered 
the survey in 2018.

00:05:00.758 --> 00:05:02.093
The researchers also looked at

00:05:02.093 --> 00:05:04.178
which of these jobs are 
at risk of automation.

00:05:04.762 --> 00:05:08.016
It turns out about 40% of 15 year olds

00:05:08.016 --> 00:05:11.769
expect to do a job at risk of automation 
in their country.

00:05:14.647 --> 00:05:16.107
When you were 15

00:05:16.107 --> 00:05:18.276
what did you expect 
you would grow up to be?

00:05:19.569 --> 00:05:23.197
It gets at this profound question 
of who you expect to be...

00:05:23.698 --> 00:05:26.367
and what you expect 
the world to look like.

00:05:27.118 --> 00:05:30.163
Maybe it's not fair to ask 
15 year olds this question...

00:05:31.164 --> 00:05:34.125
but fair or not, these surveys tell us

00:05:34.125 --> 00:05:36.794
they need more help coming up 
with their answers.

